so um  welcome to my uh talk on constant  generics  so i wanted to start by saying a little  bit about me um my name is ethan braley  i'm an a-level student from england and  i've been running russ for around three  years  i've contributed a little to  constantinex itself  inside the compiler really working on to  cite  some of the error messages so sort of  like surface level stuff  but it's been interesting to follow the  feature in its development  so i want to cover in this quick talk  as the basics of the syntax basics for  congenerics  i want to talk about um why we need cost  generics to make  arrays easier to work with i want about  a couple of applications  of cost generics the limitations of what  we have now  and then a little bit about what we can  expect in the future  one thing that you may not have noticed  before or thought about um  is the uh the fact that arrays and rust  have always been cost generic um  they take the type for the thing that  they contain  and the and the number for the length of  the array  so here i have an example a array of  five strings  and we're passing in this five as a  const parameter into our array type  on the bottom two snippets i have um  arrays using a the more  general cost generic syntax rather than  the traditional array syntax  on this side i've defined a type alias  that shows the equivalence between the  two  so on the right i have um arrays using  the traditional array syntax  and on the left i've created this alias  using constant error x  so it's generic over t and our length n  the const n u size bit is bringing a  const variable into  scope um and i kind of like the term  cons variable because it seems like a  bit of an oxymoron  how can something be constant and  variable at the same time  and i think the way to think about that  is um the same way you think about type  parameters  they are um variable within the context  of generics  because we don't know what they're going  to be as but when we use them  everything's worked out as a concrete  value um  in the monomorphization stage in the  compiler so  our constants they're constant at a  point in the compiler but before then  when before they're worked out you can  think of them as variables so it's a  const variable  so one of the things that um cos  generics enables us to do  is to write code that works on arrays of  different lengths  so being generic over all different  sized arrays  and i i think this um begs the question  why do we want to use um arrays  why can't we just use vectors  because we know the size of arrays we  can simply push them onto the stack  we don't have to allocate memory in the  same way as we do with vectors  and allocation takes time so it has a  performance  performance cost and we can also use  arrays in places where we can't use  vectors  such as embedded systems where we don't  have a  traditional allocator  i took this graph from a blog post by  adam chalmers  and it just demonstrates how  switching an implementation that uses a  vector with one that uses an array  can yield performance gains  on the left hand side here i have a  snippet from  a old copy of the standard library  and it shows how the azref trait used to  be implemented for  arrays of different sizes  here where we're calling the macro with  pass  we're using a macro here and we're  calling the macro at the bottom of the  left side  and we're passing in the numbers 0 1 2 3  all the way up to 32.  those are all of the different size  arrays that we're trying to support with  this implementation  on the right hand side we have the same  thing but written with constant generics  as you can see on the impul we're being  generic over  t the value inside our array and n the  length of our array  i think this code is a lot cleaner and  nicer to look at than  the cumbersome macro code  it's quite hard to read it also produces  a lot  nicer documentation with rust stock and  it doesn't have that limitation of only  working for arrays  up to length 32. um i wanted to talk  about this  method coming to the standard library  it's currently um  currently unstable but with  what's been stabilized in the last rest  release  it will probably be stabilized some  point in the next few releases  um it's called a ray chunks and it's a  method on slicers  that so here we have a slice of  characters and we're breaking  it up into chunks of three characters  um i think what's particularly notable  here is that  our array trunks method we're not  passing in a constant parameter at all  it's being inferred by the compiler it  knows that we want a raise of length  three  because we're comparing it with a raise  of length three  so i think this um demonstrates how um  ergonomic um are apis can be with  console generics  and we can use cost generics without  really having to  interact with it that closely it sort of  can be invisible to us  in the last rust release um what was  stabilized was a feature called  minecon's generics  um which is sort of a minimum viable  product for constant lyrics  um a lot of things are turned off  because they have quite  complex implications that haven't really  been worked out yet um  you can't use anything other than  boolean khan integer types  in your const parameters and you  um you can't do complex you can't use a  conspirator in a complex expression  so we can't take like the length of our  array and then say add one to it  because that has complex implications  um but with the nightly compiler with  unstable rust  we can unlock a a few features um like  the full consequence feature constantly  validatable  connect constant eric's defaults and  some other features  that unlock a whole set of things that  um  that aren't possible with stable rust  with constant generics  so i wanted to talk about this example i  wrote  that uses some of those nightly features  here is a way of indexing on arrays that  makes  indexing out of bounds a compiler error  in the bottom left i have um  this if enum that takes a condition as a  const parameter  and implements the trait true if that  condition is true  that allows us to write this um where  clause in the impul above  that only implements the trait if the  index is less than the length  i don't really think this is nice code i  don't think it's idiomatic rust  i think this code is quite ugly but um  i think it demonstrates  some of the power you can get from um  from the unstable features um  there are proposals on how to do on how  this might be done without the if hack  um but um this it just demonstrates  right now what what could be possible  so i think the cost generics right now  is about make writing code that's  generic over a raise of different  lengths  um but i think the future is about  allowing us to write  more um safer faster  um and better abstractions that um  can assert more things that compile time  um  i don't think it's likely that the  average uh rust developer  will um will write much constant generic  code  but i think um what you'll find if  you're when you're writing rust  that you'll find that the um the  libraries and apis you use  will use costcu lyrics to give you  better abstractions

